  "overview": "The Jetson Nano looks like a Raspberry Pi at a glance — similar 40-pin header, similar ports — but its reason for existing is the large heatsink-covered module in the middle: a genuine GPU with hundreds of CUDA cores, capable of running real-time object detection and other neural-network inference locally instead of offloading to the cloud. That makes it the board of choice for vision-heavy robotics (a robot that needs to actually recognize what it's looking at) rather than general-purpose computing. Two practical gotchas trip up newcomers: there's no built-in WiFi (you need a USB dongle or M.2 card), and Jetson's GPIO library addresses pins by physical header position ('BOARD' numbering) by default, not the GPIO/BCM numbers Raspberry Pi tutorials use — copy-pasting Pi pin numbers directly onto a Jetson usually addresses the wrong pin. The newer Orin Nano is a substantially faster successor aimed at the same niche.",
  "simpleSummary": "A small computer with a real graphics chip built in, made for running AI and vision programs.",
  "specifications": {
    "processor": "Quad-core Arm Cortex-A57, 1.43 GHz",
    "gpu": "128-core NVIDIA Maxwell GPU (CUDA-capable)",
    "operatingVoltage": "5V (board), 3.3V (GPIO logic)",
    "ram": "4 GB LPDDR4",
    "storage": "microSD card",
    "connectivity": "Gigabit Ethernet (no built-in WiFi — requires USB or M.2 adapter)",
    "usb": "4x USB 3.0, 1x Micro-USB (power/flashing)",
    "video": "HDMI + DisplayPort",
    "camera": "2x MIPI CSI-2 camera connectors (stereo vision capable)",
    "gpio": "40-pin header, Raspberry Pi form-factor, 3.3V logic",
    "connector": "Barrel jack (5V/4A) or Micro-USB (5V/2A, limited)"
  },
